Q: jQuery show/hide on click in Wordpress A: <p>In WordPress, the main issue with your code is often the <code>$</code> variable conflict and the use of incorrect jQuery methods. WordPress loads jQuery in <code>noConflict()</code> mode by default, so using <code>$</code> directly can cause issues.</p>
<p>A few tips to fix your implementation:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>No-Conflict Wrapper:</strong> Always wrap your code in <code>jQuery(document).ready(function($) { ... });</code> to use the <code>$</code> sign safely in WordPress.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid <code>href=&quot;#&quot;</code> Jumping:</strong> Use <code>e.preventDefault()</code> to stop the page from scrolling to the top when a button is clicked.</li>
<li><strong>Attribute Method:</strong> Use <code>.attr(&quot;id&quot;)</code> or <code>.data(&quot;id&quot;)</code> instead of <code>.attribute</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here's an example of corrected code:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>jQuery(document).ready(function($) {
    $(&quot;#buttons a&quot;).on(&quot;click&quot;, function(e) {
        e.preventDefault(); // Prevents jumping to the top of the page

        var id = $(this).attr(&quot;id&quot;); // Get the ID of the clicked anchor

        // Hide all content divs first
        $(&quot;#pages &gt; div&quot;).hide(); 

        // Show the specific div (e.g., #div1, #div2)
        $(&quot;#div&quot; + id).fadeIn(400); // Added a smooth fade animation
    });
});
</code></pre>
<p>For your animations, you can check out the jQuery <code>.animate()</code> documentation or use simple CSS transitions for better performance.</p>
